WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.012
Lebesgue integration begins with simple functions that take finitely many values on measurable pieces

00:00:06.012 --> 00:00:10.422
Multiply each value by the measure of its piece, then add

00:00:10.422 --> 00:00:15.633
For height two on an interval of length one, the contribution is two.

00:00:16.933 --> 00:00:21.582
A second disjoint interval of length one, with height five, contributes five

00:00:21.582 --> 00:00:31.267
Whether an endpoint belongs to the first or second piece does not affect this Lebesgue integral, because the endpoint is a set of measure zero.

00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:36.948
Combining the two measurable pieces gives total integral seven

00:00:36.948 --> 00:00:47.172
More general nonnegative measurable functions are approached from below by simple functions, and their integrals are defined by the corresponding supremum

00:00:47.172 --> 00:00:51.067
This extends the weighted-size idea beyond step-shaped graphs.

00:00:52.367 --> 00:00:59.509
For x to the n on the unit interval, pointwise convergence to zero holds except at one

00:00:59.509 --> 00:01:03.291
The functions are bounded by the integrable function one

00:01:03.291 --> 00:01:10.433
Dominated convergence therefore permits passing the limit through the integral, and the single exceptional endpoint contributes nothing.
